speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, in light of several telegrams I have received in the last few days, I am rising pursuant to Standing Order 43 on a matter of urgent and pressing necessity. On several occasions this matter has been brought to the attention of the House; I am referring to the lack of rail cars available to the lumber industry in British Columbia. The CNR has drastically cut back their supply of cars to British Columbia over the last three months, and this has now had disastrous effects since several plants have had to shut
down. Therefore I move, seconded by the hon. member for Burnaby-Richmond-Delta (Mr. Reynolds);
That this House move to strike a special committee under the Standing Committee on Transport and Communications to investigate all matters relating to this very serious situation.

Order, please. Would the hon. member resume his seat. I want to suggest that hon. members have now reverted to the first question to ask a series of supplementaries. I was hoping that today an effort would be made to go beyond three or four questions. I would invite hon. members to give the Chair an opportunity to go on to other questions before we get involved in a debate on this very important matter which was raised originally by
[Mr. Davis.}
the hon. Leader of the Opposition. The hon. member for Wellington.
